      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die, we need to break it down into the following steps:\n\n1. **Determine the probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die.**\n   - A six-sided die has six faces numbered from 1 to 6.\n   - The numbers less than three are 1 and 2.\n   - There are 2 favorable outcomes (1 and 2).\n   - The total number of outcomes when rolling two dice is \\(6 \\times 6 = 36\\).\n   - Therefore, the probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die is \\(\\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}\\).\n\n2. **Determine the probability of rolling a number greater than three on the second die.**\n   - The numbers greater than three on a six-sided die are 4, 5, and 6.\n   - There are 3 favorable outcomes (4, 5, and 6).\n   - The total number of outcomes when rolling two dice is \\(6 \\times 6 = 36\\).\n   - Therefore, the probability of rolling a number greater than three on the second die is \\(\\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\n3. **Calculate the combined probability of both events happening.**\n   - Since the rolls of the two dice are independent events, the combined probability is the product of the individual probabilities.\n   - The probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die is \\(\\frac{1}{3} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{6}\\).\n\nTherefore, the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}\\).",
