      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die, we need to analyze the outcomes of each die roll separately and then combine these probabilities.\n\nFirst, let's consider the outcome of the first die. The first die is a six-sided die, so the possible outcomes are the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6. The numbers less than three are 1 and 2. Therefore, there are 2 favorable outcomes out of 6 possible outcomes. The probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die is:\n\\[\nP(\\text{first die} < 3) = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nNext, let's consider the outcome of the second die. The second die is also a six-sided die, so the possible outcomes are the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6. The numbers greater than three are 4, 5, and 6. Therefore, there are 3 favorable outcomes out of 6 possible outcomes. The probability of rolling a number greater than three on the second die is:\n\\[\nP(\\text{second die} > 3) = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nSince the outcomes of the two dice rolls are independent, the probability of both events happening (rolling a number less than three on the first die and rolling a number greater than three on the second die) is the product of the individual probabilities:\n\\[\nP(\\text{first die} < 3 \\text{ and } \\text{second die} > 3) = P(\\text{first die} < 3) \\times P(\\text{second die} > 3) = \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{6}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}\n\\]",
